div:

In use now
         The strip at the top shows the detector in use.  Show in list finds it in the Library;
         Use none stops using any detector.

li.div:
InterSpec can use the same format DRF as used by GADRAS-DRF as long GADRAS-DRF has previously used with the DRF in order to create the Efficiency.csv file to go along with Detector.dat. Using GADRAS-DRF detector response functions has the advantage of including both intrinsic efficiency and FWHM information (which may help peak fitting). For further information on GADRAS-DRF detector response functions see: GADRAS-DRF 18.5 User’s Manual. GADRAS detector folders found in InterSpec's own folders, and in the standard GADRAS install folder, are listed in the Library automatically.
The discrete energy/efficiency pairs in the Efficiency.csv file are interpolated between using Akima interpolation. The Detector.dat file that defines the detector in GADRAS-DRF, is used to determine the FWHM information. Specifically parameter 6, 7, and 8 (denoted by P6, P7, and P8 respectively) are used according the following pseudo-code:
def getFWHM( energy ): //P6---> resolution @ E=0 (keV) //P7---> % FWHM @ 661 keV //P8---> resolution power if energy ≥ 661 or P6=0 return 6.61×P7×(energy/661)P8 if P6 < 0.0 var p = P81.0/log(1.0-P6) return 6.61×P7×(energy/661)p
if P6 > 6.61×P7 return P6;
var p = sqrt((6.61×P7)2-P62)/6.61; return sqrt(P62+(6.61×p×(energy/661)P8)2)
